    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. One issue we've been seeing in the last few test runs is that the semi-metallic pads seem to be \"acting up\" unpredictably under extreme heat. Sometimes the braking force drops off suddenly due to what looks like erratic friction changes, even when the temperature sensors are within expected ranges. Is there any physical mechanism—like gas buildup or material outgassing—that could cause this kind of erratic, almost mischievous behavior in the braking performance?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, erratic braking performance in semi-metallic pads under extreme heat is often caused by thermal running-in alongside uneven heat distribution from Glazing. During this state, the surface material transitions through phases where friction becomes unpredictable due to inconsistent contact mechanics and trapped volatile compounds. This unstable phase can lead to the sudden drop in braking force you observed, particularly when the surface temperature fluctuates near the metal's melting point. Switching to a stabilized CMC material typically eliminates this volatile window by maintaining consistent friction properties across a broader temperature range.",
      "source": "target",
